# hash_primitives_test.masm
#
# Exercises the three RPO (Rescue Prime Optimised) Poseidon2-derived
# hash primitives Miden 0.14 surfaces at the MASM source level:
#
#   * `hash`    — single-shot 1-to-1 RPO hash of a 4-felt input,
#                 producing a 4-felt digest.  19 cycles internally
#                 but surfaces as a single asmop in the recorder's
#                 cycle_idx==1 trace.
#   * `hperm`   — full 12-felt Poseidon2 permutation on the top 3
#                 words of the operand stack `[R0, R1, C, ...]`.
#                 1 cycle.
#   * `hmerge`  — 2-to-1 merge of two 4-felt digests `[A, B, ...]`
#                 -> `[C, ...]`.  16 cycles.
#
# The strict pin asserts that each hash op surfaces as a distinct
# step in the trace (the recorder aggregates the inner RPO rounds
# under a single asmop boundary -- per the spec, "the recorder
# should aggregate them under a single logical hash call_entry").
# Each hash op is wrapped in a one-line procedure so the per-op
# call_entry / call_exit boundary is the hash-call aggregation
# unit.  The output digest of `hperm` is the canonical empty-input
# Poseidon2 digest (top word of the post-permutation rate); we pin
# the four felt elements against the reference value Miden's RPO
# implementation produces for the all-zero input.
#
# Procedure layout (each op wrapped so its call_entry / call_exit
# brackets the hash invocation):
#
#   hash_op   : padw hash                         <- 1-to-1 RPO of [0,0,0,0]
#   hperm_op  : padw padw padw hperm              <- permutation of all-zero state
#   hmerge_op : padw padw hmerge                  <- 2-to-1 merge of two zero-digests
#
# All three procedures consume a known input (all zeros via
# `padw`) so the digest is deterministic and pinnable.

proc.hash_op.0
    padw
    hash
end

proc.hperm_op.0
    padw padw padw
    hperm
end

proc.hmerge_op.0
    padw padw
    hmerge
end

begin
    # Leading push so #main is the first observed asmop context.
    push.0
    exec.hash_op
    # `hash` leaves a 4-felt digest on top.  Drop it before the
    # next op so each hash invocation starts from a clean slate.
    dropw
    exec.hperm_op
    # `hperm` leaves the post-permutation state (3 words) on top.
    # Drop all 12 felts.
    dropw dropw dropw
    exec.hmerge_op
    # `hmerge` leaves a 4-felt digest.  Drop it too.
    dropw
    drop
end
